### California Ballot Propositions @california-california-ballot-propositions

#### Proposition 1 @california-proposition-1
> Housing affordability bonds
measure
placed by: Legislature
yes: Lets the state borrow $11.25 billion in general obligation bonds for
  affordable housing and veterans' home loans.
supporters: U.S. Vets, California Federation of Teachers, Habitat for Humanity
  California, carpenters' councils, Airbnb, Meta
vote: yes

Borrows money to build housing, especially the kind of housing that is difficult
to finance privately.

#### Proposition 2 @california-proposition-2
> Larger rainy day fund
measure
placed by: Legislature
yes: Changes the constitution so the state deposits more into budget reserves
  and keeps making extra debt payments for longer.
supporters: Gov. Gavin Newsom, Xavier Becerra, California Professional
  Firefighters, LA Chamber of Commerce
opponents: Asm. David Tangipa and legislative Republicans, who argue surpluses
  should go back to taxpayers
vote: yes

Makes the state save more in good years. California's budget is boom and bust,
and this helps smooth it out.

#### Proposition 3 @california-proposition-3
> Make the high-income tax surcharge permanent
measure
placed by: Initiative
yes: Makes permanent the Prop 30/55 income tax surcharge on earnings above
  roughly $360,000, which otherwise expires in 2031, to fund schools and health
  care.
supporters: California Teachers Association, California State PTA, Planned
  Parenthood Affiliates of California
opponents: California Taxpayers Association, Family Business Association of
  California, California Hispanic Chambers of Commerce
vote: yes

Keeps the existing tax on income over $360k that pays for schools and health
care. It's been in place since 2012 and should not be allowed to expire.

#### Proposition 4 @california-proposition-4
> Allow public campaign financing
measure
placed by: Legislature
yes: Repeals the state ban so the state and local governments may create public
  campaign financing programs for candidates.
supporters: League of Women Voters of California, California Nurses Association,
  ACLU of Northern California, California Democratic Party
opponents: California Taxpayers Association, Howard Jarvis Taxpayers Association
vote: no

I'm not opposed to public campaign financing. San Francisco has run a program
since 2000 and the sky hasn't fallen. The problem with Prop 4 is that it doesn't
actually establish anything. It just repeals the 1988 ban and leaves every
design question for later: whether participating candidates have to give up
private money, how many candidates can qualify, how much they can draw, and
where the money comes from. Voters would be signing off on an idea with no terms
attached, and the terms are what matter. A public financing program that still
lets candidates take special interest money alongside public money is worse than
no program at all. California has rejected this twice before, in 2000 and 2010,
and the Legislature's answer was to come back with something even vaguer. Bring
back a real proposal with a real program in it and I'll vote for it.

#### Proposition 5 @california-proposition-5
> Change the statewide recall process
measure
placed by: Legislature
yes: Removes the replacement-candidate question from statewide recall ballots,
  so a vacancy after a successful recall is filled by special election or
  appointment.
supporters: Sen. Josh Newman, League of Women Voters of California, California
  Common Cause
vote: yes

Recalls are usually anti-democratic. They let a motivated minority force a
do-over of an election the rest of us already held, at enormous cost, on a
timeline the recall backers get to choose. California's version is especially
bad because of the replacement question: a governor can be removed by 50% plus
one and then replaced by whoever leads a field of dozens with a fraction of that
support. That is how we nearly handed the state to a radio host in 2021. Prop 5
fixes the worst part. If a recall succeeds, the office is filled by a special
election or an appointment, the same way any other vacancy is, instead of by a
simultaneous free-for-all. I'd rather raise the bar for recalls altogether, but
this is a step in the right direction.

#### Proposition 37 @california-proposition-37
> State loans for middle-income homebuyers
measure
placed by: Initiative
yes: Creates a state down payment and loan assistance program for middle-income
  buyers of new homes, funded by up to $25 billion in revenue bonds repaid by
  borrowers.
supporters: California Association of Realtors, carpenters' unions, Treasurer
  Fiona Ma, Xavier Becerra, California Teachers Association
vote: yes

This one is close to free. The program is funded by revenue bonds that the
borrowers themselves pay back, not by the general fund, so taxpayers aren't on
the hook for the loans. It's opt-in on both ends: nobody has to use it, and no
builder has to participate. And it only applies to new construction, which means
every loan it makes is a unit that got built. California's housing problem is a
supply problem, and the state's middle class is the group that has been squeezed
out of ownership the hardest, so a program that nudges demand toward new homes
rather than bidding up existing ones is pointed in the right direction. I don't
expect it to move the needle much on its own, but there's no real downside. Vote
yes.

#### Proposition 38 @california-proposition-38
> Immunology research bonds
measure
placed by: Initiative
yes: Lets the state borrow $8.4 billion for immunology and immunotherapy
  research at California institutions, about half earmarked for the California
  Institute for Immunology and Immunotherapy.
supporters: Gary K. Michelson, Michael J. Fox Foundation, Alzheimer's Los
  Angeles
opponents: League of Women Voters of California, GrowSF
vote: no

The very existence of this proposition is an indictment of California's ballot
initiative system. Prop 38 borrows $8.4 billion, which costs the state $500 to
$600 million a year for twenty years, and hands half of it, about $4.2 billion,
to a single nonprofit research institute. The measure was written, qualified,
and almost entirely bankrolled by Gary Michelson, the billionaire who co-founded
that same institute. The eligibility rules are drafted so narrowly that UCLA
itself confirmed only his institute qualifies. He has put more than $60 million
into the campaign. There is essentially no money on the other side, because
there is no constituency against immunology research. That's the point: nobody
is going to run ads against curing cancer, so the only check on a ballot measure
like this is voters noticing what it actually does. It's truly insane that the
CA democratic party endorsed this measure. The party that will tell you with a
straight face that billionaires have too much influence over our politics is
endorsing a law that lets a random rich guy unilaterally loot the state
treasury. It's fucking embarrassing. Research funding should go through the
budget and through competitive grants, not through an initiative that names its
own beneficiary. If you want the state to fund immunotherapy research, this is
the most expensive and least accountable way to do it. Vote no.

#### Proposition 39 @california-proposition-39
> Voter ID requirement
measure
placed by: Initiative
yes: Amends the constitution to require government-issued ID and citizenship
  verification each time a person votes, replacing signature verification.
supporters: Reform California, Howard Jarvis Taxpayers Association, California
  Republican Party
opponents: ACLU California Action, Common Cause, League of Women Voters of
  California, California Democratic Party, SEIU 2015
vote: no

California already checks who you are when you vote. You need a driver's license
number or the last four of your social security number to register, and the
state verifies it against the DMV. Mail ballots get signature-matched.
Non-citizens voting is a felony and it basically never happens. So Prop 39
doesn't add any protection that doesn't already exist, it just adds a step at
the polls that will mostly trip up people who moved recently, lost their wallet,
or don't drive. The real reason to vote no is what happens *after* election day.
Once voter ID is in the state constitution, every close race turns into a
lawsuit about whether some county checked IDs correctly or whether a batch of
ballots should get tossed. That's the whole point. The guy bankrolling this
measure (Richard Uihlein) funds the exact same playbook in other states, and
it's not because he's worried about election security. Vote No.

#### Proposition 40 @california-proposition-40
> One-time billionaire wealth tax
measure
placed by: Initiative
yes: Imposes a one-time 5% tax on the net worth of California billionaires to
  fund health care, education, and food assistance.
supporters: SEIU-United Healthcare Workers West, California Democratic Party,
  Teamsters California
opponents: Building a Better California, California Taxpayers Association,
  California Primary Care Association, Planned Parenthood, both Becerra and
  Hilton oppose
vote: no

The thing to know about Prop 40 is that it was never really meant to pass.
SEIU-UHW, the union behind it, routinely qualifies ballot measures as bargaining
chips. You might remember these guys from their series of random dialysis
initiatives. In June the union offered to withdraw this one if Newsom backed a
smaller 2% tax through the Legislature. He declined, the deadline passed, and
the bargaining chip ended up on your ballot. That's also why the groups you'd
expect to support a health care tax, including the Medical Association, the
community clinics, and Planned Parenthood, are against it — they weren't
consulted. On the merits, a one-time wealth tax is a poor fit for a permanent
funding gap, and wealth is the easiest tax base to move, which is why the
state's own estimate comes in well under the union's and expects some ongoing
income tax loss. Income, property, and land value taxes do the same job with far
less leakage. It will almost certainly lose to a legislative challenge. If you
are truly concerned with creating a more equatable tax system, focus on
increasing property taxes and capital gains taxes, as well as closing loopholes
that impact income classification. Vote no.

#### Proposition 41 @california-proposition-41
> Audits and spending-limit rules for new special taxes
measure
placed by: Initiative
yes: Requires State Auditor review of initiative special taxes and bars
  excluding new special-tax revenue from the state spending limit. Drafted as a
  rival to Proposition 40.
supporters: Building a Better California, California Taxpayers Association,
  CalCPA
opponents: SEIU-United Healthcare Workers West
vote: no

Prop 41 is one of two measures the business lobby put on the ballot to cancel
out Prop 40 (if it gets more votes, Prop 40 dies). I'm voting no on 40 anyway,
so the question is whether 41 is good on its own, and it isn't. The part people
will miss is the Gann Limit. In 1979 voters capped how much the state is allowed
to spend each year, growing only with population and inflation, and anything
collected above the cap has to be refunded or split off to schools. That sounds
fine until you realize it means the state can't bank a windfall, can't build a
reserve, and can't catch up on infrastructure in a good year even when it has
the money. It's a big reason California's budget swings from surplus to crisis
every few years. Until now, voters could at least approve a new tax and exempt
it from the cap. Prop 41 locks that door permanently. Vote No.

#### Proposition 42 @california-proposition-42
> Ban new state wealth and retroactive taxes
measure
placed by: Initiative
yes: Constitutionally bars the state from creating new taxes on ownership of
  financial assets or other personal property, and certain retroactive taxes. A
  rival to Proposition 40.
supporters: Building a Better California, California Professional Firefighters,
  State Building and Construction Trades Council
opponents: SEIU-United Healthcare Workers West
vote: yes

This is the other anti-Prop 40 measure, and unlike 41 it's actually good policy
in a vacuum. It does two things: bans new taxes on simply owning stuff (wealth
taxes), which I covered under Prop 40, and bans retroactive taxes. The
retroactive part is the one worth caring about. Good tax policy is predictable:
you set the rules, people plan around them, and the revenue shows up on
schedule. A retroactive tax breaks that. It can't change any behavior because
the behavior already happened, so it raises no money going forward and just hits
whoever happened to be standing there when the rule changed. Worse, it's
unstable money. It gets litigated for years, the people it targets restructure
or leave, and the Legislature ends up budgeting around revenue that may never
arrive. Vote Yes.

#### Proposition 43 @california-proposition-43
> Two-thirds vote for citizen-initiative local taxes
measure
placed by: Legislature
yes: Requires two-thirds voter approval instead of a simple majority for local
  special taxes placed on the ballot by citizen initiative, closing the Upland
  loophole.
supporters: California Business Roundtable, California Taxpayers Association
opponents: California Professional Firefighters, California Federation of
  Teachers, SEIU California, building trades
vote: none

Local special taxes (ones earmarked for a specific purpose) normally need a
two-thirds vote. In 2017 the state Supreme Court ruled in the Upland case that
this only applies when the city council puts the tax on the ballot. If a citizen
initiative does it, a simple majority is enough. Since then the move has been
for whichever interest group wants money to gather signatures, write the tax so
it funds their thing, and skip the council entirely. That's genuinely bad
governance: the tax is drafted by its beneficiaries, nobody weighs it against
other priorities, and it's locked in forever. Prop 43 closes the loophole by
making initiatives clear two-thirds too. The problem is that two-thirds is a
brutal threshold, and most of the things I actually want cities to fund
(transit, housing, Muni) only pass because of the loophole. I don't love either
outcome, so I'm not taking a position. Vote your conscience.

#### Proposition 44 @california-proposition-44
> Community clinic spending requirement
measure
placed by: Initiative
yes: Requires certain private nonprofit community health clinics to spend at
  least 90% of annual revenue on direct patient care.
supporters: SEIU-United Healthcare Workers West
opponents: California Medical Association, California Primary Care Association,
  California Hospital Association, California Democratic Party
vote: no

From the people who brought us Prop 40 comes another attempt to use the
initiative process for the benefit of a single interest group. SEIU-UHW is the
only funder, and the measure is the leverage they use when they want clinic
operators at the bargaining table. Who opposes this measure? The community
clinics themselves, through the California Primary Care Association, plus the
pediatricians, the Medical Association, and the hospitals. Nobody who actually
runs a clinic thinks a hard 90% rule makes sense, because the stuff that doesn't
count as "direct patient care" is things like the billing staff that gets them
paid by Medi-Cal, the electronic records system, and the building. These clinics
run on razor thin margins serving mostly low-income people, and forcing them to
hit an arbitrary ratio or face penalties means fewer clinics, not better ones.
If the union wants a contract, it should go negotiate one. It's not the state's
job to pickup the slack. Vote No. ***In general, a good rule of thumb is that if
a ballot measure is primarily sponsored by SEIU-UHW, you should probably kill it
with fire.***

#### Proposition 45 @california-proposition-45
> Narrow CEQA review for certain projects
measure
placed by: Initiative
yes: Narrows California Environmental Quality Act review for specified public
  and private projects, with tighter deadlines and limits on court challenges.
supporters: California Chamber of Commerce, California Building Industry
  Association, California Association of Realtors, PG&E
opponents: Sierra Club California, California Environmental Voters, California
  Labor Federation, State Building Trades
vote: yes

**Weakens CEQA. CEQA is bad.**

