#### San Francisco Ballot Measures @san-francisco-san-francisco-ballot-measures
> Every local measure this year needs a simple majority to pass.

##### Proposition A @san-francisco-proposition-a
> Charter changes to City departments and commissions
measure
placed by: Board of Supervisors, 9 to 2
yes: Eliminates or reorganizes certain commissions, moves many commissions from
  the Charter into the Municipal Code, expands the City Administrator's real
  estate role, and changes how legal settlements are approved.
supporters: Mayor Daniel Lurie, as part of the "Clean Up City Hall" package with
  D, E, F, GrowSF
opponents: Coalition on Homelessness
vote: yes

Some history. In 2024 there were two competing commission reform measures: Prop
D, which would have capped the city at 65 commissions outright, and Peskin's
Prop E, which created a task force to study the problem instead. I voted for D
and called E feckless. D lost, E won, and credit where it's due, the task force
did its job. It produced the first complete inventory of every commission in the
city, which nobody had ever bothered to make, and recommended a long list of
cuts and mergers. Prop A is the Board of Supervisors acting on those
recommendations. It takes the charter from 55 commissions to 38, moving nine
into the Municipal Code where they can be changed by ordinance instead of a
ballot measure, and eliminating or merging eight more. The Board adopted a
narrower version than the task force wanted, so this is less than D would have
done, but it's real progress and it came out of a process that I didn't think
would deliver. Vote Yes.

##### Proposition B @san-francisco-proposition-b
> Municipal finance corporation and public bank
measure
placed by: Board of Supervisors, 9 to 2
yes: Authorizes the City to create a city-owned non-deposit lender that could
  later convert into a public bank after profitable operation and regulatory
  approval.
supporters: SF Public Bank Coalition, San Francisco Foundation, SF Labor Council
opponents: GrowSF and individual opponents
vote: no

##### Proposition C @san-francisco-proposition-c
> Housing Trust Fund contributions
measure
placed by: Board of Supervisors, 11 to 0
yes: Gradually raises the City's annual Housing Trust Fund contribution to $125
  million, expands eligible uses to include social housing and co-ops, raises
  the down payment loan income limit to 200% of area median income, and extends
  the fund from 2043 to 2058.
supporters: Non-Profit Housing Association of Northern California, San
  Franciscans for Social Housing, SF Democratic Party, GrowSF
opponents: SF Republican Party
vote: yes

##### Proposition D @san-francisco-proposition-d
> Changes to the ballot measure process
measure
placed by: Signature petition, sponsored by Mayor Lurie
yes: Raises the signature requirement for citizen initiatives from 2% to 8% of
  registered voters, raises the threshold for calling special elections to 10%,
  allows initiatives to be withdrawn before an election, and removes the ability
  of the Mayor or four Supervisors to place measures on the ballot.
supporters: Mayor Daniel Lurie, SF Democratic Party, Teamsters Local 665, Gov.
  Gavin Newsom, GrowSF
opponents: SF Labor Council, Senior and Disability Action, United Educators of
  SF, SF Tenants Union, Indivisible SF
vote: yes

##### Proposition E @san-francisco-proposition-e
> City Administrator authority and contracting
measure
placed by: Signature petition, sponsored by Mayor Lurie
yes: Centralizes purchasing and contracting authority under the City
  Administrator, raises the contract values requiring Board approval from $10
  million to $25 million, and extends the City Administrator's term from 5 to 10
  years.
supporters: Mayor Daniel Lurie, SF Democratic Party, Firefighters Local 798, SF
  Building Trades, GrowSF
opponents: SF Labor Council and allied groups
vote: yes

##### Proposition F @san-francisco-proposition-f
> Executive branch management
measure
placed by: Signature petition, sponsored by Mayor Lurie
yes: Expands the Mayor's authority to transfer responsibilities between
  departments, hire deputy mayors, hire and fire most department heads including
  the Police Chief, and lets appointing authorities remove most commissioners
  without cause.
supporters: Mayor Daniel Lurie, SF Democratic Party, SPUR, SF Chamber of
  Commerce, Firefighters Local 798, SEIU 87, GrowSF
opponents: Small Business Forward, DSA SF, SF Labor Council
vote: yes

##### Proposition G @san-francisco-proposition-g
> Private vehicles on the Upper Great Highway
measure
placed by: Signature petition
yes: Closes Sunset Dunes Park to recreation on weekdays and reopens the Upper
  Great Highway to private vehicles Monday 4 a.m. through Friday 6 p.m.,
  reversing part of 2024's Prop K.
supporters: Chinese American Democratic Club, SF Taxpayers Association, Richmond
  District Democratic Club, Supervisor Alan Wong
opponents: Sierra Club, SPUR, Surfrider SF, Outer Sunset Neighbors, Scott
  Wiener, GrowSF
vote: no

Puts cars back on the Great Highway. Cars are still bad.

##### Proposition H @san-francisco-proposition-h
> Parcel tax for Muni operations
measure
placed by: Signature petition
yes: Creates a 15-year parcel tax based on property type and square footage,
  from $129 per single-family parcel up to $400,000, with senior exemptions,
  raising about $177 million a year for Muni service.
supporters: SF Democratic Party, SF Labor Council, SF Chamber of Commerce, SF
  Tenants Union, Seamless Bay Area, GrowSF
opponents: SF Republican Party, Business League
vote: yes

Raises property taxes to fund public transit. My two favorite things.

##### Proposition I @san-francisco-proposition-i
> Transfer tax dedication for affordable housing
measure
placed by: Signature petition, backed by DSA SF and Dean Preston
yes: Permanently dedicates half of transfer tax revenue from property sales over
  $10 million to a new House SF Fund for affordable and social housing. Prevails
  over Prop J if both pass and conflict.
supporters: Affordable Housing Alliance, SF Tenants Union, SEIU 1021, Small
  Business Forward, DSA SF
opponents: SF Taxpayers Association, SF Building and Construction Trades
  Council, GrowSF
vote: undecided

##### Proposition J @san-francisco-proposition-j
> End the foreclosure exemption for the transfer tax
measure
placed by: Board of Supervisors, 10 to 0
yes: Starting in 2027, ends the transfer tax exemption for commercial,
  industrial, and large residential properties transferred to a lender in or to
  avoid foreclosure, for an estimated $100 million to $150 million a year.
supporters: Mayor Daniel Lurie, SF Democratic Party, SEIU 1021, SEIU 2015,
  GrowSF
opponents: SF Taxpayers Association
vote: undecided

